|---|---|---|
| **Duplicate processing** | `dequeue()` had `await asyncio.sleep(0.01)` between the `"pending"` check and the `"processing"` assignment (`job_queue.py:29`). This yielded control, letting every other worker also see the job as pending and claim it — a TOCTOU race. | Removed the spurious `await asyncio.sleep(0.01)`. Status is now set atomically before any yield. |
| **Vanishing failures** | `process_job()` caught exceptions but only printed them (`worker.py:18`) — it never called `queue.fail()`. Jobs that threw stayed stuck in `"processing"` forever, invisible to retry logic and final status reporting. | Added `queue.fail(job["id"], str(e))` in the except block so failures transition to `"pending"` (for retry) or `"failed"` (when retries are exhausted). |
